.TH CUT 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
cut \- print selected parts of lines
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cut
.RB { \-b | \-c }
.I list
.RB [ \-nD ]
.RB [ \-O
.IR sep ]
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B cut
.RB { \-f | \-F }
.I list
.RB [ \-d
.IR sep ]
.RB [ \-nsD ]
.RB [ \-O
.IR sep ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cut
reads each
.I file
a line at a time and writes the selected parts of every line: bytes with
.B \-b
or
.BR \-c ,
or fields with
.B \-f
or
.BR \-F .
Exactly one of these four options is required, and it may be given only
once.
Its argument is a
.IR list ,
described below.
.PP
With no
.I file
operands, or for a
.I file
named
.BR \- ,
.B cut
reads standard input.
A
.I file
that cannot be opened is reported on standard error and skipped, and the
remaining files are still read.
.PP
A line ends at an LF.
A CR directly before the LF is removed, so files with CRLF and LF line
endings give the same result.
A NUL byte also ends a line, and is not written.
Lines may be of any length.
Every output line ends with an LF, even when the last input line has
none.
Each input line produces one output line, which is empty when nothing in
the line is selected.
The exceptions are the lines dropped by
.BR \-s ,
and the line selection described under
.BR "Selecting lines" .
.SS Lists
A
.I list
is one or more ranges separated by commas, such as
.BR 1,4\-6,9\- .
Positions are counted from 1.
A range is one of:
.TP
.I n
Position
.IR n .
.TP
\fIn\fB\-\fIm\fR
Positions
.I n
through
.IR m ,
where
.I m
is not less than
.IR n .
.TP
\fIn\fB\-\fR
Position
.I n
and every position after it, to the end of the line.
.TP
\fB\-\fIm\fR
Positions 1 through
.IR m .
.PP
Each number is a string of decimal digits, with no sign or space, and
at most 2147483647.
Position 0 is not valid.
A malformed or too large number, and a range that starts at 0 or ends
before it starts, are errors.
An empty list, an empty range as in
.BR 1,,2 ,
and a lone
.B \-
are usage errors, which print the usage message.
Positions past the end of the line select nothing.
.SS Bytes
With
.B \-b
or
.BR \-c ,
which are the same, the list selects byte positions.
Bytes are not decoded, so a range can split a UTF-8 character.
The bytes are written in order of position, each once, so overlapping
ranges are merged.
Nothing is written between ranges unless
.B \-O
is given.
Then its argument is written between selected ranges that are not
adjacent in the line.
.SS Fields
With
.BR \-f ,
a line is split into fields at each occurrence of the delimiter byte.
That is the first byte of the
.B \-d
argument, and TAB by default.
A line with
.I n
delimiters has
.IR n +1
fields, any of which may be empty.
The selected fields are written in order, separated by the output
delimiter.
That is the
.B \-O
argument if given, otherwise all of the
.B \-d
argument, otherwise TAB.
.PP
A line that has no delimiter is written whole, whatever the list says,
unless
.B \-s
drops it.
A line that has a delimiter always produces a line of output, which is
empty when the line has too few fields.
.PP
With
.BR \-F ,
the
.B \-d
argument is instead an extended regular expression, and each match of it
in a line is a delimiter.
The default is
.BR [[:space:]]+ ,
a run of white space.
With it, white space at the start of a line makes field 1 empty.
Fields are numbered as with
.BR \-f ,
with these differences.
.IP \(bu 2
Empty fields are counted but never written, and a range whose text is
empty writes nothing, not even an output delimiter.
.IP \(bu 2
A range \fIn\fB\-\fIm\fR is written as one piece of the line, from the
start of field
.I n
to the end of field
.IR m .
The delimiters between those fields are kept as they are in the input.
.IP \(bu 2
The default output delimiter is one space.
.PP
The expression is matched against bytes, so
.B .\&
matches one byte of a UTF-8 character.
The anchors
.B ^
and
.B $
never match.
An expression that matches the empty string, such as
.BR x* ,
cuts the line into empty fields.
.PP
The ranges of a list are sorted by their starting position, so fields
are written in line order.
With
.B \-f
and
.BR \-F ,
ranges that overlap, or name a field twice, do not work reliably: they
can write a field that no range names, or an extra output delimiter.
Give ascending ranges that do not overlap, or use
.BR \-D .
.SS Selecting lines
With
.BR \-f ,
when the first byte of the
.B \-d
argument is LF, the list selects lines instead of fields.
Lines are numbered from 1 in each file.
The selected lines are written in file order, separated by the output
delimiter, which by default is all of the
.B \-d
argument, and the last is followed by an LF.
A file with no selected lines produces no output.
.B \-s
has no effect.
With
.BR \-D ,
lines are still written in file order, and a range that is out of order
in the list can select nothing.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-sd: ,
and may follow the
.I file
operands.
An option's argument may be attached, as in
.BR \-d: ,
or be the next argument.
The long option may be abbreviated to any unique prefix, and its
argument may follow an
.BR = ,
as in
.BR \-\-out=+ .
.B \-\-
ends the options.
.TP
.BI "\-b " list
Select the bytes at the positions in
.IR list .
.TP
.BI "\-c " list
The same as
.BR \-b .
Multibyte characters are not recognized.
.TP
.BI "\-d " sep
Set the input delimiter for
.B \-f
or
.BR \-F .
The default is TAB for
.BR \-f ,
and
.B [[:space:]]+
for
.BR \-F .
With
.BR \-f ,
only the first byte of
.I sep
delimits fields, and an empty
.I sep
never matches, so that every line has no delimiter.
If that byte is LF, the list selects lines.
It is an error with
.B \-b
or
.BR \-c .
.TP
.BI "\-f " list
Select the fields in
.IR list ,
split at a single-byte delimiter.
.TP
.BI "\-F " list
Select the fields in
.IR list ,
split at matches of a regular expression.
.TP
.B \-s
Drop lines that have no delimiter, instead of writing them whole.
Empty lines have no delimiter, and are dropped.
It is an error with
.B \-b
or
.BR \-c .
With
.BR \-D ,
only empty lines are dropped.
.TP
.B \-D
Do not sort the ranges.
Each range is applied to the line on its own, in the order given, so
that fields can be reordered or written twice: the output of
.B \-D \-f3,1
is field 3 and then field 1.
With
.B \-f
or
.BR \-F ,
a line that has no delimiter is then not written whole.
It has one field, field 1, so a list that does not select field 1 gives
an empty line.
.IP
With
.B \-b
or
.BR \-c ,
each byte is still written at most once, in the order of the first
range that selects it.
The
.B \-O
delimiter is written before a byte whose preceding byte in the line has
not yet been written, but only after an earlier range has ended before
the end of the line, and never before byte 1.
So on the line
.BR abc ,
.B "\-D \-O: \-b3,2"
writes
.BR c:b ,
while
.B "\-D \-O: \-b3,1"
writes
.BR ca .
.TP
\fB\-O\fR \fIsep\fR, \fB\-\-output\-delimiter\fR \fIsep\fR
Write
.I sep
between the selected fields, byte ranges, or lines.
It may be empty.
The default for
.B \-f
is all of the
.B \-d
argument, or TAB without
.BR \-d .
For
.B \-F
it is one space, and for
.B \-b
and
.B \-c
it is nothing.
.TP
.B \-n
Accepted and ignored.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the arguments, including option arguments, are expanded
against file names at startup, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
arguments as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When set, the first
.I file
operand ends the options, so that later arguments such as
.B \-f
are file names.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted, and the console interprets the
bytes in its own code page.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An error: an unknown option, an invalid list, a missing or repeated
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-f ,
or
.BR \-F ,
or
.B \-d
or
.B \-s
without
.B \-f
or
.BR \-F ;
an invalid regular expression; a
.I file
that could not be opened; or a failure writing standard output.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe whose reader exited while
.B cut
was writing.
Output still buffered when
.B cut
finishes is written as it exits, and a broken pipe found then is instead
reported as a write error, with status 1.
.PP
Errors are reported on standard error, beginning with
.BR cut: .
An unknown option, a missing or repeated list option, and an empty list
or range also print the usage message there.
The remaining files are still read after one cannot be opened.
.SH EXAMPLES
Print the first and seventh colon-separated fields:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ echo root:x:0:0:root:/root:/bin/sh | cut \-d: \-f1,7
root:/bin/sh
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print the value of each
.B KEY=VALUE
line, keeping any further equals signs in the value, and skipping lines
without one, which would otherwise be written whole:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cut \-s \-d= \-f2\- settings.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Treat each run of spaces as one delimiter:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf \(aqa  b   c\en\(aq | cut \-F2,3
b c
.EE
.RE
.PP
Reorder fields:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ echo a:b:c | cut \-d: \-D \-f3,1
c:a
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print lines 2 through 3 of a file, using the shell's
.B $\(aq\en\(aq
for an LF:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cut \-d $\(aq\en\(aq \-f2\-3 file.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print the first three bytes of each line:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cut \-c1\-3 file.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print every field from the second on, separated by commas:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ echo one two three | cut \-d\(aq \(aq \-f2\- \-O,
two,three
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU cut:
.IP \(bu 2
.BR \-F ,
.BR \-D ,
the short form
.BR \-O ,
and line selection are additions.
.IP \(bu 2
There is no
.BR \-z ,
no
.BR \-\-complement ,
and no long options but
.B \-\-output\-delimiter
and
.BR \-\-help .
A NUL byte ends a line.
.IP \(bu 2
GNU cut rejects a
.B \-d
argument of more than one character.
Here only its first byte delimits fields, and all of it is the default
output delimiter.
.IP \(bu 2
A list cannot contain spaces.
.IP \(bu 2
Overlapping ranges for
.B \-f
are not merged reliably; see
.BR Fields .
.PP
Text is handled as bytes, in whatever encoding it has.
A non-ASCII delimiter for
.B \-f
splits at its first byte, so use
.B \-F
for it, which matches the whole byte sequence.
Cut UTF-8 text by field where possible, not by byte position.
.PP
Files and the standard streams are binary, so the C runtime translates
no line endings.
.B cut
removes the CR of each CRLF itself, so input CRLF becomes LF in the
output.
To write CRLF line endings, pass the output through
.BR unix2dos (1).
.PP
File names may use drive letters and backslashes.
They may contain any Unicode characters on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later, and are limited to the system code page on earlier versions.
The device names
.IR /dev/null ,
.IR /dev/zero ,
.IR /dev/urandom ,
.IR /dev/stdin ,
and
.I /dev/tty
(the console) work as on Unix.
A directory operand fails with
.BR "Is a directory" .
.PP
When standard output is a console, bytes above 0x7F are converted for
display from the ANSI code page, which is UTF-8 on Windows 10 version
1903 and later, to the console's code page.
.PP
From
.BR cmd.exe ,
wildcard characters in an argument, even a quoted one or that of
.B \-d
or
.BR \-O ,
are expanded against file names unless
.B BB_GLOBBING
is
.BR 0 ,
so a delimiter of
.B *
or
.B ?\&
can be replaced by file names.
A pattern that matches no file is left as it is.
Commands run from the kit's shell normally get no such expansion; see
.B BB_GLOBBING
under
.BR ENVIRONMENT .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR awk (1),
.BR busybox (1),
.BR grep (1),
.BR paste (1),
.BR sed (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR sort (1),
.BR unix2dos (1)
